# Commercial S3.1 state setgid/resume abort

Date: 2026-08-29

The first state-repair invocation stopped before creating the replacement
cursor. The observed parent directory began as `chatops:chatops` mode `2700`.
GNU chmod preserves set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits on directories unless they
are explicitly cleared, so `chmod 0700` left the parent at `2700`. The
Application ownership guard correctly returned
`S3_STATE_OWNERSHIP_OR_MODE_INVALID`.

Before that guard, the controller had already preserved the numeric Telegram
offset in the valid recovery directory, removed the archived root-owned cursor
and removed the archived empty legacy `media` directory. No replacement cursor
was created. The only live state children are the unchanged chatops-owned
mode-0700 `evidence` and `media-test` directories. The database, unit and
service lifecycle were not changed; the service remains inactive/dead with
`NRestarts=0` and no process.

The correction explicitly executes `chmod g-s` and verifies mode `0700` before
calling Application state initialization as `chatops`. It also adds a narrowly
bounded resume path for exactly this interrupted shape. Resume requires the
preserved offset file, extracts the old cursor from the already validated
recovery archive, compares both offsets and rejects every other live path. It
does not accept a generic missing cursor and performs no ownership takeover.
